declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-102';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 5, $j$2.1 The Ascension and the Day of Pentecost$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Acts 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Acts/1"}, {"title": "Acts 2 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Acts/2"}], "body": "Read Acts 1 and Acts 2 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nThe book of Acts was written by Luke as a second volume to his Gospel. It tells how the good news spread from Jerusalem to Rome. Before Jesus returned to heaven, He told His followers to wait in Jerusalem for the promise of the Father and said that they would receive power and be witnesses. He was taken up, and a cloud received Him out of their sight. About one hundred and twenty believers waited and prayed. On the day of Pentecost they were all filled with the Holy Ghost and spoke with other tongues as the Spirit gave them utterance. Peter preached, the crowd asked what to do, and about three thousand souls were added to the church that day.\n\nKey words: Acts, witness, Pentecost, Holy Ghost, repentance.\n\nYour task: (1) Tell who wrote Acts and what the book is about. (2) Write Acts 1:8 in your own words. (3) Tell what Peter told the crowd to do in Acts 2:38 and how many people were added that day.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "Welcome to Unit 2. The four Gospels showed us the life of Jesus. Now we open the book of Acts, which shows what Jesus continued to do through His church. Acts was written by Luke, the same man who wrote the Gospel of Luke, and it tells how the good news spread from Jerusalem all the way to Rome.\n\nBefore Jesus went back to heaven, He told His followers to stay in Jerusalem and wait for the promise of the Father. Then He gave them a mission. Acts 1:8 says, But ye shall receive power, after that the Holy Ghost is come upon you: and ye shall be witnesses unto me both in Jerusalem, and in all Judaea, and in Samaria, and unto the uttermost part of the earth. That verse is like a table of contents for the whole book. First Jerusalem, then Judaea and Samaria, and then the ends of the earth.\n\nRight after He said this, Jesus was lifted up, and a cloud received Him out of their sight. Two men in white told the disciples that this same Jesus would come again. The believers went back to an upper room, about one hundred and twenty of them, and they prayed together with one accord.\n\nThen came the day of Pentecost, a Jewish feast fifty days after Passover when Jerusalem was full of visitors. Acts 2:4 says, And they were all filled with the Holy Ghost, and began to speak with other tongues, as the Spirit gave them utterance. People from many nations heard the wonderful works of God in their own languages.\n\nPeter stood up and preached. He told the crowd about Jesus, whom they had crucified and whom God had raised from the dead. The people were pricked in their heart and asked what they should do. Acts 2:38 says, Repent, and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ for the remission of sins, and ye shall receive the gift of the Holy Ghost. About three thousand souls were added that day. The church was born.\n\nYour task is to tell who wrote Acts, put Acts 1:8 in your own words, and tell what Peter told the crowd to do and how many were added. Then take the four question check.\n\nThe same Spirit who came at Pentecost is the promise of the Father for us today. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$2.1 The Ascension and the Day of Pentecost$j$);
end
$seed$;
